A||||12||donny||cost of a 2.8||||1||
Z||000000||donny||11-04-2001||04:03 PM||||Can anyone help me out I bought an 86.5 gt<BR>in close to mint condition with only 64k original miles for $750.I thought it was quite a good deal.Anyway I'm wondering how much you guys think I could get a lower mileage 2.8 for.Any help is appreciated.<BR>   thanks,donny||||reg||1
Z||000001||gtsonly||11-04-2001||05:52 PM||||The car only has 64,000 miles on it, you want a 2.8 with lower miles???????????????<BR>You lost me on this one, is the engine blown?   <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/myfiero001_copy_1.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000002||donny||11-04-2001||05:54 PM||||Yeah I'm sorry I forgot to mention the most important part the engine is blown.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||11-04-2001||06:21 PM||||Actual cost will vary. Another used engine, or a remanufactured engine or a new crate engine.  Choices.<P>A used working 2.8L engine $50-500 depending on condition.  Potential downside its used and old.<P>A rebuilt engine professionally done. $1200-1500.  Do it yourself $500. Do you have the skills to do a rebuild and installation?<P>New crate 3.1L or 3.4L engine $2000-2500. <P>You'll have to figure on installation time or cost.<P>I don't know where Drum, Pa is but Waynes World of Fieros is in Allentown, 610 797 0521 a resource.  Check out the fiero web ring for Waynes current information.<P>Check out the vendor list for engine work.  If it were me (I did it), bite the bullet and for about $4000, you can get a new 3.4L crate engine with all new components installed.  So if the car is in good shape, you'll have a real nice reliable 86.5 GT and as a 5 speed one of limited production numbers. Where else can you get a nice car for under $5K.<P> The downside is being without a car for a while and the good places that do these swaps are in Illinois (Twin Lakes Fiero) or Alabama (Fiero Factory).  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Borgio||11-04-2001||06:23 PM||||Put a 3.4L in it. You can get one from a 93-95 camaro or firebird. They are a direct bolt in. Few mods are needed. You may be more likely to find a lower mileage one. While you have the engine out, also, port the exhaust manifold. Then it can look stock and you'll also have some extra horsepower.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fropuff||11-04-2001||06:25 PM||||If you can find a 3.4L and have someone put it in for me, you can take my '86 2.8L with......64,000 original miles!  What a frickin coincidence.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||donny||11-04-2001||06:30 PM||||Thanks for the reply.I originally wanted a five speed but for the price and convenience(car is only 10 minutes away from my house)I took it.My dad's friend can usually get an engine in good shape for around 100 dollars so I'll probably end up going that way.I'll check with him first.But thanks for the good info.||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnF||11-04-2001||06:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by donny:<BR><B>Yeah I'm sorry I forgot to mention the most important part the engine is blown.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>YeaH, I've got one with that "pesky little problem" also.  My Fireo knowledgeable, professional offered to get a good used Baretta (under 60K miles and warranteed) for $700 and to swap it out for about the same.  Total cost about $1400+ incidentals (maybe another $150 to $200).<P>Ed Parks of the Fiero Factory will send you a completely rebuilt engine for about $1350 on a pallet.  You install.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||donny||11-04-2001||06:37 PM||||I would go with a 3.4 but I'm aiming at keeping the car stock.If I come across a gt that's not as clean and original as this one I'll probably buy that one and put a larger engine in that one.Once again thanks for the replies.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Old Lar||11-04-2001||06:40 PM||||The 3.4L can have all the Fiero intake and valve covers put on.  It will look pure stock.  Only your mechanic will know.||||reg||1
Z||000010||rigdonhome||11-04-2001||06:54 PM||||I will have a 2.8 out of an '85 S10 soon.  Runs good but has more than 64,000 miles on it.  (Not sure exactly how many yet... don't have the truck yet!)  Will probably be asking $200 for it, but I'm in Indiana!<P>Cheers,<BR>Scott<P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000011||watts||11-04-2001||07:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Borgio:<BR><B>Put a 3.4L in it. You can get one from a 93-95 camaro or firebird. They are a direct bolt in. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Except for having to redrill the block for putting the starter on the other side.  Not quite a bolt in.  Close, but...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||watts||11-04-2001||07:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rigdonhome:<BR><B>I will have a 2.8 out of an '85 S10 soon. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Remember that the 2.8's pre 86 had smaller journal's (thus even weaker bottom ends!) than the 86 & up models.  I know this is correct for the S-10's, any others I'd just be assuming are the same situation.<BR>||||reg||1
